//@version=6
// #157 R10 — the trade-list PARITY vehicle.
//
// Deliberately the simplest strategy that can close trades in BOTH engines, because the point
// is to compare the engines, not to test a strategy. Every gate that would make one side empty
// is therefore absent:
//
//   * NO `barstate.isrealtime` gate. The l2b vehicle has one, and its own header states the
//     consequence: "warmup never trades (a plain backtest places ZERO = proof)". That makes l2b
//     structurally unusable for this comparison — the backtest arm is empty by design, so a
//     parity assertion on it would be 0 == 0 and would pass no matter how wrong the fake was.
//   * NO trade window. winStart/winEnd are absolute wall-clock times, and against a replayed
//     past day any window either opens during warmup or never opens at all.
//   * NO conditional orders. A market entry exercises the path both engines share; the
//     conditional lifecycle is pinned directly against the venue in the conformance suite.
//
// One lot, pyramiding 0, enter-once-per-flat, close after a fixed number of bars, so the trade
// list is a deterministic function of the bar series alone.
strategy("R10 parity vehicle", overlay = true,
         default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
         pyramiding = 0, initial_capital = 10000000000,
         calc_on_every_tick = false)

holdBars = input.int(3, "Bars to hold")

// Enter on a green bar whenever flat. Market, so it fills in the backtest at the next bar's
// open and on the fake at the next print, which is exactly the difference the tolerance covers.
if strategy.opentrades == 0 and close > open
    strategy.entry("E", strategy.long, comment = "parity entry")

// Close after holdBars bars. bar_index arithmetic only, so both engines see the same trigger.
if strategy.opentrades == 1 and (bar_index - strategy.opentrades.entry_bar_index(0)) >= holdBars
    strategy.close("E", comment = "parity exit")

plot(close, "close")
